ELTEC Eurocom E17 Linux port - status & plan
============================================

Base: the 030funtime MMU+device-tree line (setup_mm.c selects an
embedded DTB per machine; boards keep a small config.c for machine id,
early console and IRQ setup, while the DT drives the peripherals).
Modelled on MVME147 (the closest VME MMU board).

Hardware (reverse-engineered; see E17-NOTES.md in the qemu-e17 tree):
  MC68040/060, VMEbus, 0xfec00000 onboard I/O window.
  CD2401 serial (0xfec64000), Am79C900/LANCE (0xfec68000),
  NCR 53C720 SCSI (0xfec6c000), Z8536 CIO timers (0xfec10000/30000),
  M48T02 RTC/NVRAM (0xfec20000), VIC068A interrupt controller
  (0xfec01000), AT keyboard, onboard video.

Done
  - arch/m68k/dts/{mc68040.dtsi,eltec-e17.dts} (embedded DTB), validates.
  - arch/m68k/eltec/config.c: machine id, early polled CD2401 boot
    console, user-vector IRQ setup.
  - machine wiring: MACH_E17, VME_TYPE_E17, Kconfig ELTEC_E17,
    setup.h MACH_IS_E17, setup_mm.c dispatch + DTB fallback + bootinfo.
  - eltec-e17_defconfig; full vmlinux builds and links.

Boot status (tested in QEMU -M e17 -kernel vmlinux)
  The kernel boots through head.S, MMU/paging, DT unflatten and early
  start_kernel, reaching calibrate_delay with NO faults.  Fixed along
  the way: the embedded-DTB pointer (&symbol, not its first word), and
  unflatten_device_tree() must run after paging_init() (memblock has no
  RAM before that) - reordered in setup_mm.c.  The QEMU e17 machine now
  builds an m68k bootinfo (MACH_E17, CPU/FPU/MMU 68040, memchunk,
  cmdline) for -kernel.

Next (boot bring-up, roughly in order)
  1. Timer tick: calibrate_delay spins waiting for jiffies.  Wire a
     tick source (Z8536 CT3 via the VIC, or a LEGACY_TIMER_TICK) so
     time advances.
  2. Console output: the early CD2401 console (config.c) produces
     nothing yet.  The E17 CD2401 TX goes through the VIC068A / the
     0xfec660fb ack (as the qemu model and RMON use), not a plain TDR
     write - implement that polled TX so the boot banner appears.
  3. VIC068A irqchip driver + wire the DT interrupts (LIRQ1=CIO tick,
     LIRQ6=CD2401).
  4. A real CD2401 tty driver (mainline dropped cyclades) for the
     login console; LANCE and 53C720 already have mainline drivers.
  5. Userspace / rootfs (initramfs first, then SCSI or NFS root).

SMP (the hard part - honest assessment)
  m68k Linux has NO SMP support today, and the dual-68040 has no
  hardware cache coherency between CPUs and no hardware IPI beyond the
  shared-DRAM + 0xfec58000 release we found.  Plan: bring the second
  CPU up as a controlled AP (reset vector at DRAM 0/4, release via
  0xfec58000 - proven with the qemu mailbox probe), then build the
  minimum SMP scaffolding (CAS-based spinlocks, per-cpu, an IPI over
  a VIC/shared-mem doorbell, explicit cache management at coherency
  points).  Full symmetric scheduling may not be reachable on this
  hardware; progress will be reported honestly as it goes.
